   27	 * Moves the claim to the status the action leads to; refuses a change its status does not allow, and an open,
   28	 * settle or void by a role the sheet's permission matrix does not allow it to. Opening records
   29	 * who opened the claim; it takes no number. A claim is numbered when it is settled (or approved): the sheet's next number in the fiscal year of its due-by (FY<year>-<n>, shared across the sheet's tree) (or, while the sheet's period numbering is on, the next in its period: <period>-<n>,
   30	 * X-<n> for a claim in no period); a claim numbered already keeps its number, and a voided claim keeps its number (never reused). The one who opened a
   31	 * claim cannot settle it, and a clerk (any name not made a steward) settles a claim only when its due is at or below
   32	 * the clerk limit in force (its person's grade's on the sheet's today when one is set, else the sheet's; a due of n/a is within any limit; no limit when the sheet has none): a clerk settling one
   33	 * above it leaves it PENDING instead. Only a steward, or its person's approver, approves a PENDING claim (it is settled, its due fixed at its live
   34	 * figure then) or rejects it, with a reason that is not blank (it is OPEN again); the one who opened it cannot approve it. The one who last edited a claim (its form or its amount entry) cannot settle or approve it either. Settling fixes the claim's due at the rate of the grade its
   35	 * person had on the claim's date, in force on the claim's date, at least the sheet's floor (or at the figure its person's last statement bound it to), plus its late charge on the sheet's today (none when no rate applies then, so it stays live). A claim deferred until after the sheet's today cannot be settled. A claim whose due is above the proof threshold in force on the claim's date (its person's grade's on that date when one is set, else the sheet's) cannot be settled or approved while it has no proof, nor while the sheet's proof kinds are set and it lacks a proof of one of them — refused before the clerk limit is considered. A claim whose due is above the sheet's note threshold in force on the claim's date cannot be opened while its note is empty. A claim whose due is above the sheet's review threshold in force on the claim's date goes to REVIEW when opened instead of OPEN: it records who opened it. Only a steward reviews a REVIEW claim, which opens it (OPEN). A claim whose due-by is in a closed period cannot change status. A claim whose due-by is in a period with a cap cannot be opened while its person already has that many OPEN or PENDING claims whose due-by is in it. A DRAFT is deleted (DELETED: it counts nowhere and
